What changed on this repo
================================================================================
## Add 60Hz interpolation
The game will run at 60 fps on display presentation, but the internal framerate 
changed from 35 fps to 30 fps. So the game difficulty (Game Speed) is around 
Slow and Normal Game Speed.

## Removed player inertia
The ship player now will move and stop like normal shmups.

## Adjusted keyboard speed
It is using value 5. The same as ref repo :
  https://github.com/Yakkers17/opentyrianlinearmovement

## Zinglon missing frame effect fix
This occured probably from 60Hz modification, fixed.

## Clouds are transparent on High Detail
Clouds (background 2) now blend 50/50 with whatever is behind them on every
detail level, so enemies under a cloud stay visible. Previously High Detail
forced opaque clouds, which looked like a transparency bug but was the original
Tyrian behaviour (only Pentium and above had `wild` set). High Detail also now
keeps the 60 Hz presentation, which it used to disable.

## Bullet Hell mode
Setup - Gameplay - Bullet Hell (ON/OFF), off by default. Each enemy volley is
fanned into three rings 14 degrees apart, so an authored one-shot pattern opens
into a roughly 28 degree arc with the original pattern down the middle, and the
fire rate is raised as well. Bullet speed is untouched: the mode gets its
difficulty from density and pattern shape, which is also what makes it readable.
The setting is stored as `bullet_hell` under `[gameplay]` in `opentyrian.cfg`.

Bullet Hell turns Small Hitbox on, because it is not survivable on the stock
hitbox. Turning Small Hitbox back off turns Bullet Hell off with it, so the two
rows can never disagree with what is actually in effect.

Combat score is worth more in this mode: 2x on Easy/Normal, 1.5x from Hard to
Maniacal, and 1.25x above that. The extra is kept separate from your cash, so
the shop budget and the galaga life threshold are unaffected, and it does not
feed the score that raises the difficulty between levels. It shows on the score
at the end of a level and on the high-score table, not on the in-game counter.
It is not saved, so reloading from a shop menu drops what you had earned.

The rate it adds shrinks as difficulty rises, so that it does not compound with
the halvings the game already applies above Normal and Maniacal. Measured
against stock at a weapon firing every 40 ticks, total bullet density is about
5.7x on Easy/Normal, 8.6x on Hard through Maniacal, and 10.9x on Zinglon and
above. Difficulty can also be raised mid-level by level event 46, so the
density shifts during a battle.

The difficulty menu shows only Easy, Normal and Hard by default. The rest are
unlocked in the menu itself: hold Shift+G for Impossible, Shift+] for Suicide,
and type L-O-R-D for Zinglon. Those three are the only way to select the higher
tiers; Insanity, Maniacal and Nortaneous only ever appear through the
between-level difficulty rise or a level script.

`--regress-bullet-count` reports enemy bullet density per tick: mean and peak
shot-pool occupancy, mean and peak drawn shots, and how many volleys were
truncated because the pool was full. It is the number to check when changing
the tuning constants in `src/varz.h`.

## Smooth motion can be turned off
Setup - Graphics - Smooth Motion. On (the default) presents at the display
refresh rate by interpolating between logic ticks; off restores the original
single-frame path. The setting is stored as `smooth_motion` under `[video]` in
`opentyrian.cfg`. It has no effect on Nonstandard VGA, which never enables it.

Known bugs
===============================================================================
- Blank on playfield. Press ESC to bring up menu should fixed it, otherwise 
restart the game.
- Blank on options menu, restart the game.

The 60 Hz presentation shows a replay of the recorded frame rather than the
framebuffer itself, so a replay bug shows up directly on screen. To find one,
run with `--regress-interp-check`: it renders every tick through the
interpolated renderer at alpha = 1 and reports the first pixel that differs
from the real frame. `--regress-replay-check` does the same via the plain
replay path.

Results go to `drawlist-regress.log`, next to `opentyrian.cfg` in the working
directory. It is written as frames are checked, not only when you quit, so a
crash or a hard kill still leaves you something to read. The game is a
GUI-subsystem binary, which is why this file exists: `stderr` is thrown away
unless you redirect it by hand.

OpenTyrian2000
================================================================================

OpenTyrian2000 is an open-source port of the DOS game Tyrian. It is a fork of
OpenTyrian, with the end goal being to replicate the experience of Tyrian 2000
as closely as possible.

Tyrian is an arcade-style vertical scrolling shooter.  The story is set
in 20,031 where you play as Trent Hawkins, a skilled fighter-pilot employed
to fight MicroSol and save the galaxy.

Tyrian features a story mode, one- and two-player arcade modes, and networked
multiplayer.

== Additional Necessary Files ==================================================

OpenTyrian2000 requires the Tyrian 2000 data files, which have been released
as freeware:
  https://www.camanis.net/tyrian/tyrian2000.zip

== Keyboard Controls ===========================================================

alt-enter      -- toggle full-screen

arrow keys     -- ship movement
space          -- fire weapons
enter          -- toggle rear weapon mode
ctrl/alt       -- fire left/right sidekick

== Network Multiplayer =========================================================

Currently OpenTyrian2000 does not have an arena; as such, networked games must
be initiated manually via the command line simultaneously by both players.

syntax:
  opentyrian2000 --net HOSTNAME --net-player-name NAME --net-player-number NUM

where HOSTNAME is the IP address of your opponent, NUM is either 1 or 2
depending on which ship you intend to pilot, and NAME is your alias

OpenTyrian2000 uses UDP port 1333 for multiplayer, but in most cases players
will not need to open any ports because OpenTyrian2000 makes use of UDP hole
punching.

Note that Network play has not been tested for OpenTyrian2000.

